# Example snapshot discipline

Every ladder example commits its emitted C and JS and expected stdout so the
lowering is reviewable in diffs and drift is caught:

- `examples/0N-name/generated.user.c` - the C user lowering (post-prelude)
- `examples/0N-name/generated.user.js` - the JS user lowering (post-prelude)
- `examples/0N-name/expected.txt` - exact stdout of the built binary
- `examples/prelude.reference.c` - the C runtime prelude, byte-identical
  across every example, stored once
- `examples/prelude.reference.js` - the JS runtime prelude, byte-identical,
  stored once

`out.kira.c` itself is gitignored. `out.kira.c = prelude.reference.c +
generated.user.c` (same split for JS).

## Minification

Generated user layers are **minified + obfuscated by default** (`OutputMinifier`):
comments/whitespace stripped, user identifiers renamed, `main` + extern/opaque
names preserved, prelude untouched and readable. The committed snapshots are
the minified form; `--readable` / `build.minify: false` produce the pretty
form for inspection. Never hand-edit generated.user.c / generated.user.js /
expected.txt.

## The rule

Any change that affects emitted C/JS (KiraCCodeGenerator.kt,
KiraJSCodeGenerator.kt, OutputMinifier.kt, CMagicTypeLowering.kt,
CIntrinsicsTable.kt, c_generator.c, c_bundle.h, js_generator.js,
kira/*.kira stdlib surface) MUST regenerate and commit snapshots in the same
change:

```bash
./gradlew installDist          # rebuild CLI with the change
./examples/regenerate.sh       # refresh snapshots, build, and run every example
./examples/regenerate.sh --check  # CI mode: fail if anything is stale
```

- `--check` re-runs each binary and diffs stdout, so it catches behavior
  changes, not just text drift.
- regenerate.sh also fails if the runtime prelude stops being byte-identical
  across examples (that is what makes the single reference copy honest).
- CI runs `regenerate.sh --check` and `examples/ffi-mini/run.sh`; a backend
  change without refreshed snapshots breaks CI.
- If a snapshot diff is purely mechanical from a deliberate lowering change,
  regenerate and commit it.
